# tests/rib

## sphere.rib

The runtime baseline. Not third-party.

## quadrics.rib

All seven RenderMan quadrics -- Sphere, Cone, Cylinder,
Hyperboloid, Paraboloid, Torus, Disk -- in one scene, each exercising its own
`getLocation`/`getNormal` parameterization end to end (tessellation, shading,
silhouette), not only the isolated finite-difference proof
`tests/normals_test.cpp` already covers. Not third-party.

## screenwindow.rib

`RiScreenWindow`, `RiCropWindow` and `RiFrameAspectRatio`
together under perspective: an asymmetric `ScreenWindow` shifting the visible
frustum off-centre, then `CropWindow` rendering only a sub-window of that
already-shifted result -- the combination `orthowindow.rib` and
`cropwindow_test.cpp` test in isolation but not together.
`FrameAspectRatio` has no tokenizer entry; included here to prove it is
warned-and-skipped, not a parse desync. Not third-party.

## clipping.rib

A cylinder parallel to the camera's z axis, far longer than
the scene's `[near,far]` `Clipping` range, seen in profile: `GMANPolygonClipper`
must cut it cleanly at both ends, producing a finite visible band rather than
the whole tube, a degenerate sliver, or a crash. Not third-party.

## shaders.rib

The three shipped surface shaders -- matte, plastic, metal --
side by side under identical lighting, so a regression confined to one
shader's own math shows up as a change confined to its own sphere. Not
third-party.

## patch_norender.rib

A `"bilinear"` `Patch` beside a control `Sphere` under identical
lighting and shading -- the defect `tests/patchnorender_test.cpp` pins.
`getRSPatch` wires bilinear and bicubic `Patch` to `createParametric`.
The `Sphere` proves the renderer itself works and the
pixels are the `Patch`'s own. No golden minted -- bilinear interpolation of
a flat four-point patch has no shading detail worth pixel-regression
protection. Not third-party.

## patch_bicubic.rib

A `"bicubic"` `Patch`, under `Sides 1` and a lit `"Kd"`: the
point set is the same whether the sixteen-point control net is read in
RISpec's u-fastest/v-major order or transposed, so `Sides 2` or `Ka`-only
shading cannot tell them apart -- a transposed reading culls to nothing
here instead. A trailing `Sphere` follows AGENTS.md's "RIB authoring"
section, "The desync convention." Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bilinear.rib

`PatchMesh "bilinear"`, nonperiodic on both axes: nu=3 nv=2, two patches
side by side sharing their middle column of control points -- the
smallest grid that exercises sub-patch selection rather than degenerating
to a single `Patch "bilinear"`. A trailing `Sphere` follows AGENTS.md's
"RIB authoring" section, "The desync convention." Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bilinear_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bilinear"`, periodic on both axes: an outer and inner diamond
ring of control points, each wrapping its last strip back to control
point 0 -- the case `createParametric` samples all the way to u=1.0 and
v=1.0, where a mesh's wraparound indexing is most likely to read out of
bounds. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_bicubic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"`, nonperiodic on both axes: nu=7 nv=4 under the
default Bezier basis, two sub-patches in u sharing their middle column.
Control points are evenly spaced and coplanar, so `GMANBasis::bicubicMesh`
must trace the same flat rectangle a bilinear mesh would; any curvature in
the render would mean a sub-patch boundary picked up the wrong control
points. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_bicubic_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"`, periodic on both axes: nu=6 nv=3, three concentric
hexagonal control rings, exercising `GMANBasis::offset`'s wraparound on
both axes at once. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_mixed_wrap.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"` with `uwrap "periodic" vwrap "nonperiodic"`: a
hexagonal tube, two periodic sub-patches around and one nonperiodic
sub-patch along its length. `uwrap` and `vwrap` are independent tokens --
the varying parameter count is (patches on the periodic axis) times
(patches+1 on the nonperiodic axis), not one axis's rule applied to both;
this is the only fixture that can tell that formula apart from a
collapsed all-or-nothing one, via an explicit `"Cs"` sized to the count
it implies. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_baddim.rib

Syntactically well-formed, semantically invalid: nu=5 does not align to
the default Bezier basis's step of 3. Registered in
`tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, not `tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp` -- that file
is scoped to tokenizer-level malformation and asserts only "does not
crash," neither of which this is. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bspline_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"` under a `Basis "b-spline" 1 "b-spline" 1` (step=1),
periodic on u at nu=3 -- the tightened periodic floor
`validPeriodicBicubicMeshDim` requires at step=1 (every other bicubic
fixture here uses the default Bezier basis, step=3, where the old,
looser bound was already tight enough to be safe). Regression guard: the
fix must still accept and correctly render every periodic mesh a step=1
basis can legally form at its minimum. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_bspline_periodic_baddim.rib

The step=1 counterpart to `malformed/patchmesh_baddim.rib`: nu=1
periodic under a `Basis "b-spline" 1 "b-spline" 1`, one below
`validPeriodicBicubicMeshDim`'s true floor at step=1. Against the
unfixed validator (`n>=step` alone) this read one point past a 4-point
`"P"` array -- `GMANBasis::offset`'s periodic wraparound is a single
`i-=nu`, not a true modulo, and only stays in range for nu>=3 at
step=1. Registered in `tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback as `patchmesh_baddim.rib`. A trailing
`Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_short_p.rib

`nverts` sums to more vertices than `"P"` holds -- `GeneralPolygon`'s own
short-`"P"` case, `patchmesh_short_p.rib`'s counterpart. Registered in
`tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`, asserting the same RIE_CONSISTENCY stop.
A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/pointsgeneralpolygons_short_p.rib

`verts`' own point count (1 + its max index) exceeds what `"P"` supplies --
`PointsGeneralPolygons`' own short-`"P"` case, `pointspolygons_short_p.rib`'s
counterpart. Registered in `tests/pointspolygons_test.cpp`, asserting the
same RIE_CONSISTENCY stop for `PointsGeneralPolygons`' own supplied
counts, which no other fixture checks. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_negative_nverts.rib

A negative `nverts` entry: `RiGeneralPolygonV` rejects it before sizing
the parameter list, warns naming the request and the offending value, and
adds the empty stub to the world instead of indexing `"P"` by a negative
count. Registered in `tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_empty_nverts.rib

An empty `nverts` array -- `nloops` is its length, so this is `nloops = 0`.
Registered in `tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_zero_step_basis.rib

`Basis "b-spline" 0 "b-spline" 0` ahead of a bicubic `PatchMesh`:
`RiBasis` takes `ustep`/`vstep` on faith, and `RiPatchMeshV`'s
`nupatches`/`nvpatches` divide `nu`/`nv` by that step before
`getRSPatchMesh` ever validates it. Registered in
`tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, asserting the same warn-and-`create()`
fallback as `patchmesh_baddim.rib`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_dimensions_overflow.rib

A bilinear `PatchMesh` of 65536 by 65536 points: `nu * nv` is 2^32, past
`RtInt`'s range. The count saturates at `INT_MAX`, so `"P"`'s element count
exceeds `RtInt`'s range and the file stops with `RIE_RANGE`. Registered in
`tests/ribcountbounds_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/pointspolygons_declared_vertices.rib

`verts` reaches vertex 2000000, so `"P"` declares 6000003 floats and supplies
nine. The file stops with `RIE_CONSISTENCY` before `"P"` allocates. Registered
in `tests/ribcountbounds_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_declared_nverts.rib

`nverts [4 1500000]` declares a hole loop of 1500000 vertices for a `"P"` of
four points -- `GeneralPolygon`'s sum-of-`nverts` count against a short array.
Stops with `RIE_CONSISTENCY`. Registered in `tests/ribcountbounds_test.cpp`. A
trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_declared_dimensions.rib

A bilinear `PatchMesh` of 2000 by 1500 points, whose product fits `RtInt`,
over a `"P"` of four points. Stops with `RIE_CONSISTENCY`. Registered in
`tests/ribcountbounds_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/pointspolygons_short_p_by_one.rib

`"P"` declares nine floats and supplies eight: a shortfall of one value is an
error, as a shortfall of millions is. Stops with `RIE_CONSISTENCY`.
Registered in `tests/ribcountbounds_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/paramliteral_surface_well_formed.rib

The control for `paramliteral_surface_string_number.rib`: the same `Surface`
with `"string mode" ["fast"]`, a string for a string declaration. It renders,
logs no error and carries all three parameters to the shader. Registered in
`tests/paramliteraltype_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## nupatch_bilinear.rib

An order-2x2 `NuPatch` (`nu = nv = 2`, knots `[0 0 1 1]` on both axes) --
a clamped degree-1 B-spline, mathematically bilinear interpolation of its
own four control points, the same as `patch_bilinear_twin.rib`'s
`Patch "bilinear"` over the same points in the same order. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, rendered and compared pixel by pixel against its
own twin. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patch_bilinear_twin.rib

`nupatch_bilinear.rib`'s own twin: the same four control points in the
same order, issued as a plain `Patch "bilinear"`. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## patch_bicubic_curved.rib

One curved `Patch "bicubic"` under the default Bezier basis, the sixteen
control points bulging at the corners and sagging in the middle, lit by
`ambientlight` and `distantlight` under `plastic` and filling about a quarter
of the frame. `tests/patchraycoverage_test.cpp` renders it under the
z-buffer and the ray renderers and compares the pixels each covers; a
renderer that draws no `Patch` fails every one. A trailing `Sphere` follows
the desync convention. Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bspline_tube.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"` under a `Basis "b-spline" 1 "b-spline" 1`, periodic on
u (nu=4) and nonperiodic on v (nv=5): a four-sided tube rotated 55 degrees
about x so its inside and outside both show. Lit by `ambientlight` alone with
`"Ka" [1] "Kd" [0]`, so every fully covered pixel has one flat colour in
every renderer, which `tests/patchraycoverage_test.cpp` compares as well as
coverage. The periodic seam must close without a gap. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## nupatch_texturecoordinates.rib

Pins the out-of-scope decision that `RiTextureCoordinates` never changes
a `NuPatch`'s render: `getRSNuPatch` always passes `kIdentityCorners`
regardless of what was supplied. An order-2x2 `NuPatch` over the same
camera, lighting, `Sides 2` and paintedplastic/checker-texture surface as
`tests/rib/texcoords/patch_texturecoordinates.rib`, carrying that
fixture's own corner transpose. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting this fixture and `nupatch_texturecoordinates_default.rib`
render bit-for-bit the same. Not third-party.

## nupatch_texturecoordinates_default.rib

`nupatch_texturecoordinates.rib`'s own twin, with no
`RiTextureCoordinates` at all. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`.
Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_short_uknot.rib

`uknot` one value short of the declared `nu + uorder`. The RI signature
carries no knot-array length, so `parseNuPatch` checks it itself and
calls nothing rather than handing `RiNuPatchV` a short array. Registered
in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the stop: `RIE_CONSISTENCY` at
`RIE_ERROR`, exit 1, and a trailing `Sphere` that never parses. Not
third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_long_vknot.rib

`vknot` one value past the declared `nv + vorder`, with a correct `uknot`:
the long, v-side twin of `nupatch_short_uknot.rib`. `parseNuPatch` stops
the file rather than handing `RiNuPatchV` a long array. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same stop. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_decreasing_knots.rib

`uknot` is the declared length but not non-decreasing. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the warn-and-`create()` fallback. A
trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_order_exceeds_n.rib

`uorder = nu + 1`: two control points cannot support a cubic span.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_empty_range.rib

`umin == umax`. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the
same warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_umin_below_knot.rib

`umin < uknot[uorder-1]`. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting the same warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_short_pw.rib

`"Pw"` two values short of its declared `nu*nv*4`. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting `GMANParameterList`'s shortfall error,
`RIE_CONSISTENCY`, fires and stops the file (before `RiNuPatchV`'s own
weight-positivity check ever runs). A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_zero_weight.rib

A full-length `"Pw"` array with one control point's own weight `<= 0`.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the new
weight-positivity warning. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_v_order_exceeds_n.rib

`vorder = nv + 1`: the v twin of `nupatch_order_exceeds_n.rib`'s own u
rule. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_v_decreasing_knots.rib

`vknot` is the declared length but not non-decreasing: the v twin of
`nupatch_decreasing_knots.rib`'s own u rule. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same warn-and-`create()`
fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_v_empty_range.rib

`vmin == vmax`: the v twin of `nupatch_empty_range.rib`'s own u rule.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_vmin_below_knot.rib

`vmin < vknot[vorder-1]`: the v twin of `nupatch_umin_below_knot.rib`'s
own u rule. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_vmax_above_knot.rib

`vmax > vknot[nv]`, the v twin of `nupatch_umax_above_knot.rib`'s own u
rule. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_umax_above_knot.rib

`umax > uknot[nu]`: the third u range rule. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same warn-and-`create()`
fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_rule_order.rib

Breaks both a uknot non-decreasing rule and the vorder rule at once, so
the request violates two rules that fire at different points in
`validateNuPatch`'s own sequence. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting the vorder warning fires and no uknot warning ever prints. A
trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_illegal_block.rib

A `NuPatch` issued inside `FrameBegin`/`FrameEnd` before any
`WorldBegin` -- the "F"-only block state `cmdNuPatch`'s mask excludes.
`allowed(cmdNuPatch)` reports `RIE_ILLSTATE` at `RIE_WARNING` and
`RiNuPatchV` returns before its own validation runs, after
`parseNuPatch`'s knot arrays are already owned, proving the parser frees
them when the request is ignored. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting exit status 0 and one `WARNING: RIE_ILLSTATE`. No trailing
`Sphere`, since the file has nothing to render. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_singular_ctm.rib

`Scale 1 0 1` collapses the y axis, so `createParametric`'s own
`invert()` throws `RIE_MATH` on a well-formed `NuPatch`. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting exit status 1, `RIE_MATH` in the
output, and no `LeakSanitizer` report, proving the primitive's stack
transform survives the throw. No trailing `Sphere`, since the throw
unwinds before one would parse. Not third-party.

## rotate.rib

An in-world `Rotate` must compose ahead of the transforms declared after it,
about the world origin, not the camera (`AGENTS.md`, "Handedness and matrix
convention"; `tests/rotate_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## polygon.rib

A single convex pentagon (five vertices, not four -- a 4-vertex
fixture is not enough to pin `RiPolygonV`'s parameter-list sizing)
under one ambient light, so the raster coverage and the
shaded color both have an independently hand-computable value
(`tests/polygon_test.cpp`). `Clipping 0.5 50` per `AGENTS.md`'s RIB
authoring section: a camera-facing planar polygon is exactly the flat,
narrow-in-z geometry the near-clip precision defect hits hardest. Not
third-party.

## polygon_general_hole.rib

Written for the `GeneralPolygon` hole-bridging task: a square with a square
hole cut from its centre, `polygon_concave.rib`'s shape and camera. Not
third-party.

## r4_raytracer.rib

`lights.rib`'s scene (ambient plus distant light,
`plastic`, a unit sphere at `Translate 0 0 5`, 640x480) under its own
`Display` name, so `tests/rayrender_test.cpp` can render it under `-r
gmanraytracer` and again with the default z-buffer without the two runs
colliding on one output file. Not third-party.

## shadow.rib, shadow_nolight.rib, shadow_noblocker.rib

A small sphere sits between a distant light and a large receiving
sphere (`tests/shadowrender_test.cpp`), plus an ambient
light so the cast shadow is not pure black. `shadow_nolight.rib` is the
same scene with the distant light removed, and `shadow_noblocker.rib` the
same scene with the small sphere removed; the test pins one pixel inside
the shadow against both. Not third-party.

## cornellbox.rib, cornellbox_direct.rib

A closed box, red and green side walls placed symmetrically about the
point light near its ceiling, matte throughout with `Ka` = `Kd`, naming
the radiosity pass: colour bleeds from each side wall onto the back
wall beside it, a bleed `tests/cornellbox_test.cpp` measures as a
back-wall asymmetry rather than against a rendered reference, since the
scene's own symmetry makes the two sides comparable directly.
`cornellbox_direct.rib` is the same scene naming no pass, where that
asymmetry disappears. Dimensions are this fixture's own, not the
published Cornell Box's. Not third-party.

## radiositywindow.rib, radiositywindow_opaque.rib, radiositywindow_direct.rib

A closed room whose one glazed wall is four opaque polygon sections
framing a polygon pane, lit by a distantlight outside aimed steeply
downward: its rays can reach the floor through the pane but never the
ceiling, whose inward normal faces away from any light arriving from
above. Naming the radiosity pass, the lit floor's indirect bounce
brightens the ceiling; `radiositywindow_opaque.rib` raises the pane's
`Opacity` to fully opaque, admitting no light at all; naming no pass,
`radiositywindow_direct.rib` shows the ceiling reading whatever ambient
light alone gives. `tests/radiositywindow_test.cpp` samples one ceiling
strip across all three, isolating the pass's own contribution from the
pane's opacity and from the ray tracer's own direct-light transmission.
Not third-party.

## string_escape.rib

Written for the RIB string-escape task: an `Attribute "user" "note"` array
holding `"a \" b"` -- an escaped quote inside a bracketed string -- followed
by a `Sphere`. Before `GMANRIBTokenize::parseString` decoded escapes, the
unescaped `"` closed the string early, the array never saw its `]`, and the
parse desynced from there on; registered in `tests/ribstringescape_test.cpp`,
which reads the decoded value back through a `GMANRenderManImpl` subclass
overriding `RiAttributeV`, as well as running it end to end. Not
third-party.

## pointscount.rib

Three `Points` requests for `tests/pointscount_test.cpp`: a 6-float `"P"`
(two points), a `"P"`-less request carrying a 3-float `"Cs"` (a
multiple-of-three parameter under a different name, guarding against a
construction that matches the first parameter regardless of name), and a
4-float `"P"` (not a multiple of three). A trailing `Sphere` follows. Not
third-party.

## ribparsefallback.rib

The 14 requests `GMANRIBParse` dispatches counts-aware on a
`GMANRenderManImpl`, issued against a renderer that is not one, for
`tests/ribparsefallback_test.cpp`'s in-process check of the RI-mandated
fallback overload each receives. `Polygon` carries a 9-float `"P"`. A
trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/

Hand-written fixtures for malformed RIB input. `gman` is expected to fail on
every one of these; the point in each case is *how* it fails -- cleanly, with
no leak and no hang, not with a crash or an infinite loop.

| File | Exercises |
|---|---|
| `display_badtype.rib` | `parseDisplay` copies `"name"` before it validates `"type"` -- a bad token in `"type"`'s place must not leak the already-copied `"name"`. |
| `stringarray.rib` | A string array with a non-string element, via `TokenVector::toRtTokenArray` -- must release the strings it already duplicated before throwing. |
| `truncated.rib` | A RIB stream cut off mid-keyword, no trailing newline -- the tokenizer's main read loops must terminate cleanly at end of input rather than looping or reading past the buffer. |
| `unbalanced_bracket.rib` | A bracketed array with no closing bracket -- `GMANRIBParse::parseArray` must stop at EOF rather than consuming the rest of the file looking for a `]` that never arrives. |
| `unterminated_string.rib` | A plain (non-array) string with no closing quote, running to EOF -- `GMANRIBTokenize::parseString` must terminate cleanly at end of input. |
| `declare_array_overflow.rib` | A `Declare` array-size digit string that overflows even `long` -- `gman::InlineParse::is_int` must reject it rather than let `strtol` saturate to a garbage quantity that sizes a later `new RtFloat[]`. Not tokenizer-level like the rest of this table; shares the harness because the bar is the same, see `tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp`. |
| `string_trailing_backslash.rib` | A string whose last character before end of file is a backslash -- an incomplete escape, not a literal character. `GMANRIBTokenize::decodeEscape` must stop cleanly at end of input here, exactly as `unterminated_string.rib` does for the unescaped case. |

Each is run under a hard wall-clock timeout (`tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp`),
independent of ctest's own `TIMEOUT` property, since a tokenizer hang on
malformed input is exactly the failure a test-level timeout alone might not
catch cleanly.

## requests/

Minimal, hand-written fixtures, one per RISpec 3.2 request with
parse-and-ignore coverage (`ifelse.rib` covers the four conditional requests
together; `solid.rib` covers `SolidBegin`/`SolidEnd` together -- neither pair
is meaningful RIB in isolation). `pixelfilter.rib` covers `PixelFilter`.
`generalpolygon.rib` covers `GeneralPolygon`, a request with a
variable-length `nverts` array ahead of its parameter list -- the same
desync exposure a mis-counted parse anywhere else in the front end has.
Each follows AGENTS.md's
"RIB authoring" section, "The desync convention."

## nonewline/

Hand-written fixtures for the tokenizer's end-of-input defect: a token that
runs to end of input with no trailing delimiter had its last character
duplicated (`WorldEnd` became `WorldEndd`). `top.rib` is a top-level file
ending `WorldEnd` with no trailing newline; `parent.rib`/`child.rib` and
`parent_gz.rib`/`child.rib.gz` are the same defect through a plain and a
gzip'd `ReadArchive` target, `child.rib` itself ending `AttributeEnd` with no
trailing newline.

## ribshaderrequests_*.rib

`tests/ribshaderrequests_test.cpp`'s fixtures: one scene (a sphere under an
ambient and a distant light), varied by one `Atmosphere`, `Displacement` or
`Imager` request. `ribshaderrequests_control.rib` carries no such request.
Each `_missing.rib` names a shader no plugin provides, so the request stops
the file with `RIE_NOSHADER`; each `_loaded.rib` names a test plugin
(`countingvolume`, `identitydisplacement`, `countingimager`) with a one-entry
parameter list and renders the control's pixels. `Atmosphere` and
`Displacement` sit in the world block before the `Sphere`; `Imager` sits
before `Projection`. Not third-party.

## texturewarningonce.rib

A `paintedplastic` square filling a 128x128 frame, naming a texture no file
carries (`tests/texturewarningonce_test.cpp`), in one world block, then a
trailing `Sphere` out of frame. Not third-party.

## texturewarningonce_frames.rib

The same scene twice, in two `FrameBegin`/`FrameEnd` blocks, each writing its
own `Display` name (`tests/texturewarningonce_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## textureopacity_cutout.rib

A red `paintedplastic` card in front of a green matte backdrop
(`tests/textureopacity_test.cpp`). The card names an opacity map, written by
the test, that is 0 over its left half and 1 over its right, so the backdrop
shows through the left half. A distant light aimed along +x and +z lands the
card's shadow clear of the backdrop pixels behind the left half. Not
third-party.

## textureopacity_nomap.rib

`textureopacity_cutout.rib`'s scene with the card naming no opacity map: both
halves of the card stay red. Not third-party.

## textureopacity_shadow.rib

A green matte receiver lit by a distant light, and a `paintedplastic` card
outside the frame, placed to shadow the receiver left of centre were it opaque
(`tests/textureopacity_test.cpp`). The card names an opacity map that is 0
everywhere. Not third-party.

## textureopacity_shadow_nomap.rib

`textureopacity_shadow.rib`'s scene with the card naming no opacity map: the
card is opaque and shadows the receiver. Not third-party.

## textureopacity_shadow_nocard.rib

`textureopacity_shadow.rib`'s scene with the card removed: the receiver
unshadowed. Not third-party.

## shadowterminator.rib

A coarse eight-sided prism, open at both ends, its vertices on a circle with
radial vertex normals, under one distant light that grazes the face nearest the
camera from the side it turns away from
(`tests/shadowterminator_test.cpp`). The shading normal faces the light across
a band of that face while the flat face does not. Not third-party.

## shadowterminator_blocked.rib

`shadowterminator.rib`'s prism with a wall at x = 1.2, outside the frame and
edge-on to the camera, between the light and the prism. The wall shades the
whole prism (`tests/shadowterminator_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## shadowterminator_backside.rib

The far half of such a prism, seen by the camera and lit by a distant light
from the side opposite its vertex normals
(`tests/shadowterminator_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## zbuffervertexnormals.rib

A coarse eight-sided prism, open at both ends, its vertices on a circle with
radial vertex normals, under one distant light along the camera axis
(`tests/zbuffervertexnormals_test.cpp`). The interpolated normal at every point
of a face is radial, so each pixel's brightness is exact arithmetic. Not
third-party.

## zbuffervertexnormals_reversed.rib

`zbuffervertexnormals.rib`'s prism with each quad's indices reversed, so every
face normal points into the prism while its vertex normals still point out
(`tests/zbuffervertexnormals_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## zbuffervertexnormals_hole.rib

A square face with a square hole, every vertex normal, the hole's included,
tilted 60 degrees away from the light axis of one distant light along the
camera axis, under a `ShadingRate` so coarse that every triangle dices once and
every shaded vertex is a corner (`tests/zbuffervertexnormals_test.cpp`). A
corner or hole vertex shaded with the face's normal instead of its own
brightens the pixels beside the hole. Not third-party.

## imagerbackground_control.rib

A camera-facing `Disk` of radius 1 under one distant light along the camera
axis, shaded uniformly by `matte` to its silhouette, with no imager. The
reference image for every `imagerbackground` fixture, whose edge pixels hold a
bright premultiplied colour at partial coverage
(`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## imagerbackground.rib

`imagerbackground_control.rib` under `Imager "background" "background" [0.2 0.3
0.4]` (`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## imagerbackground_default.rib

`imagerbackground_control.rib` under `Imager "background"` with no parameters,
whose backdrop is white (`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## imagerbackground_exposure_control.rib

`imagerbackground_control.rib` under `Exposure 2 1.8`, with no imager
(`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## imagerbackground_exposure.rib

`imagerbackground.rib` under `Exposure 2 1.8`, which exposes the covered colour
and leaves the backdrop as the imager sets it
(`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## imagerbackground_bgcolor.rib

`imagerbackground_control.rib` under `Imager "background" "color bgcolor" [0.5
0.5 0.5] "background" [0.4 0.6 0.8]`, the form Aqsis's scenes pass, whose
backdrop is the product of the two colours (`tests/imagerbackground_test.cpp`).
Not third-party.

## creasestreak.rib

One `PointsPolygons` of two faces under one distant light along +x: face A,
facing a little away from the light, whose vertex normals tilt toward it and
converge along the ridge, and face B, folded back from the ridge and facing the
light. A band of A has its shading normal facing the light across the fold
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_open.rib

`creasestreak.rib`'s face A alone, the unoccluded reference for its terminator
band (`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_reversed.rib

`creasestreak.rib` with every face wound against its normals
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_blocked.rib

`creasestreak.rib` plus, in the same mesh, a wall at x = 1.2 with normal +x,
outside the frame. The wall is the second crossing from face A's band and the
first from face B's (`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_far.rib

`creasestreak_open.rib`'s face A plus, in the same mesh, a card at x = 3 that a
shadow ray crosses from behind, farther than face A's own size
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_facing.rib

`creasestreak_open.rib`'s face A plus, in the same mesh, a card at x = 0.3
facing -x, which a shadow ray crosses from the front
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_other.rib

`creasestreak_open.rib`'s face A plus a separate smooth `Polygon` card at
x = 0.3 facing +x, which a shadow ray crosses from behind within face A's own
size (`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_backside.rib

One mesh of two faces: a quad seen and lit from behind its normals, and a card
between the quad and the light that a shadow ray crosses from behind
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## creasestreak_backside_open.rib

`creasestreak_backside.rib`'s quad alone, the reference for the card's shadow
(`tests/creasestreak_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## normalmap_card.rib

A `paintedplastic` card facing the camera under one distant light tilted along
the card's `dPdu` (`tests/normalmappathtracer_test.cpp`). The card names a
normal map, written by the test, whose texel tilts the shading normal along
`dPdu`. The card is the only geometry in view, so the path tracer's estimate
there is its light sample alone. Not third-party.

## normalmap_card_nomap.rib

`normalmap_card.rib`'s scene with the card naming no normal map. Not
third-party.

## normalmap_bounce.rib

A `paintedplastic` card naming the same normal map, lit by bounce light alone
(`tests/normalmappathtracer_test.cpp`). A distant light shines on a matte wall
beside the camera and reaches only the card's back, so every ray of light the
card shows is a BSDF-sampled bounce off the wall. Not third-party.

## normalmap_bounce_nomap.rib

`normalmap_bounce.rib`'s scene with the card naming no normal map. Not
third-party.

## bumpterminator.rib

A half-prism of five vertical quads, open, its vertices on a circle with radial
vertex normals, under one distant light that grazes the face nearest the camera
(`tests/bumpterminator_test.cpp`). The surface is `paintedplastic` naming a
normal map, written by the test, whose texel tilts the shading normal toward +x
along `dPdu`. The shading normal faces the light across a band of that face
where the interpolated normal does not. Not third-party.

## bumpterminator_nomap.rib

`bumpterminator.rib`'s scene with the prism naming no normal map. Not
third-party.

## bumpterminator_bounce.rib

A `paintedplastic` card facing the camera, its normal map tilting the shading
normal toward a wall behind the card's plane, with a distant light travelling
along +x parallel to the card (`tests/bumpterminator_test.cpp`). The wall lies
outside the frame; no light reaches the card directly, so every ray of light
the card shows arrives by a BSDF-sampled bounce off the wall. Not third-party.

## outofmode_control.rib

A lit sphere, 32x32, and no request out of mode: the image `outofmode_imager.rib`
and `outofmode_option.rib` must match pixel for pixel. Registered in
`tests/outofmode_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## outofmode_imager.rib

The control with `Imager "background" "background" [1 1 1]` after `WorldBegin`,
the shape the Aqsis Blobby examples take. `Imager` is legal only outside the world
block, so it reports `RIE_ILLSTATE` at `RIE_WARNING` and is ignored; the file
renders the control's exact pixels under one warning. The `Sphere` that follows
the request follows the desync convention. Registered in `tests/outofmode_test.cpp`.
Not third-party.

## outofmode_option.rib

The control with `Exposure 0.5 1` after `WorldBegin`. The output reads the gain
when it writes, so an applied `Exposure` would halve every channel of the control's
pixels; ignored, it leaves them exact, under one `RIE_ILLSTATE` warning. The
`Sphere` that follows the request follows the desync convention. Registered in
`tests/outofmode_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## outofmode_block.rib

An `AttributeBegin` inside `ObjectBegin`, which `enterMode(A)` excludes. A block
request out of place still stops the file: one `WARNING: RIE_ILLSTATE`, exit 1, no
image. Registered in `tests/outofmode_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## arealight_panel.rib

Two `Polygon` panels side by side under one `AreaLightSource` over a `Kd` 0 matte
surface, the left wound to light the camera and the right wound away from it. Under
`gman -r gmanpathtracer` the left panel reads the light's `intensity` times its
`lightcolor`, held under 1 so 8-bit output never clips, and the right reads black:
a polygon emits one-sided along its winding's normal. The block ends on a `Sphere`
outside the frame, which follows the desync convention. Registered in
`tests/arealightpanel_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## rayarealight_sphere.rib

A matte wall lit by a sphere emitter above and in front of it, out of frame, with a
black sphere between them casting a soft shadow. The emitter and the blocker are
black and the wall is one plane, so the path tracer renders direct light alone. At 64
draws per shading point the ray tracer's mean over the lit block (columns 24-39, rows
4-11) agrees with the path tracer's at 64 paths within 1.5% and over the penumbra block
(columns 26-37, rows 16-27) within 4%. Registered in `tests/rayarealight_test.cpp`,
whose copies of it also prove the draw-count option and the warning for an emitter the
ray tracer skips. Not third-party.

## rayarealight_disk.rib

The sphere fixture with a `Disk` emitter turned to face the wall by `Rotate 180 0 1 0`.
The ray tracer's lit and penumbra blocks agree with the path tracer's within the same
1.5% and 4%. Registered in `tests/rayarealight_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## rayarealight_panel.rib

The sphere fixture with a square `Polygon` emitter of the disk's area, wound to emit
toward the wall. The ray tracer's lit and penumbra blocks agree with the path tracer's
within the same 1.5% and 4%. Registered in `tests/rayarealight_test.cpp`. Not
third-party.

## rayarealight_glass.rib

The panel fixture with a clear glass slab, two `Polygon` faces 0.1 in front of the
emitter, out of frame, covering every path from the emitter to the visible wall. Light
reaches the wall through both faces, so the lit block reads about 0.92 of the panel
fixture's under each renderer (two faces at index 1.5 pass 0.9216 at normal
incidence), and the ray tracer's lit block agrees with the path tracer's within 1.5%.
Registered in `tests/rayarealight_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## raycomposite_glass.rib

A matte wall lit by a distant light, a black matte panel of `Opacity` 0.5 in front of
it, and a clear glass slab of two `Polygon` faces in front of the panel's left half. The
panel reads on the right of the frame directly and on the left through the slab, so the
ray tracer's block through the slab (columns 6-21, rows 12-35) must read half of its
bare twin's and agree with the path tracer's within 1.5%, while the direct block (columns
42-57, rows 12-35) reads half of the twin's. The light sits where no shadow ray from the
wall crosses the panel or the slab. Registered in `tests/raycomposite_test.cpp`. Not
third-party.

## raycomposite_glass_bare.rib

The glass fixture without the panel: the wall alone, seen directly and through the slab.
Its through-slab block reads about 0.92 of its direct block (two faces at index 1.5),
and each block of the panel fixture reads against it. Registered in
`tests/raycomposite_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## raycomposite_mirror.rib

A matte wall lit by a distant light, the same black `Opacity` 0.5 panel in front of it
and a 45 degree mirror, so the camera sees the panel and the wall in the reflection. The
ray tracer's block (columns 54-61, rows 14-33) must read half of the bare twin's and
agree with the path tracer's within 1.5%; the block starts at column 54, where the path
tracer's wall-mirror interreflection no longer lifts its reading. Registered in
`tests/raycomposite_test.cpp`. Not third-party.

## raycomposite_mirror_bare.rib

The mirror fixture without the panel: the wall alone in the mirror, the reference the
panel's block reads against. Registered in `tests/raycomposite_test.cpp`. Not
third-party.

## raycomposite_ambient.rib

A green wall and a translucent red `Opacity` 0.5 sphere before it, lit by an ambient
light, seen directly and in a `Kr` 0.8 mirror. The ray tracer alone renders it, since the
path tracer reads no `ambientlight`. The sphere's front face, back face and the wall
read together in the direct block (columns 16-19, rows 22-25), and the mirrored block
(columns 48-51, rows 22-25) reads 0.8 times it in every channel, within 1.5 levels, so a
wrong `Kr` and a wrong `Cs` read apart. Registered in `tests/raycomposite_test.cpp`. Not
third-party.

## corpus/

Real RIB from third-party software, fetched once and checked in so tests are
hermetic (no network at test time).

| File | Source | License | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `menger.rib` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/procedurals/menger/menger.rib`, commit `d258e4ee5200ef29a512f2f4237b06fa3e4bb853` | GPL-2.0 (repo license; this file itself carries no separate header) | The corpus test (`tests/ribdialect_test.cpp`). Shader-free, so it is one of the few Aqsis examples that reaches `RiWorldEnd` without hitting a missing shader plugin. Exercises `Procedural "DynamicLoad"` and the unbracketed `"fov" 45` parameter form. |
| `bike.rib`, `bikeData.rib.gz` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/features/archives/`, commit `a9ecc18a9ccbddb418b3ad3940c98a541e427461` (model courtesy of jrMan, www.jrman.org, per the file's own header) | GPL-2.0 (repo license) | Exercises `ReadArchive` of a gzip'd file in the same step, over ~5,300 lines of real `Patch`/`Basis`/`TransformBegin` content -- the two hardest step-6/step-7 paths together. A real `plastic` shader closes the shading-infrastructure gap this file's first `Surface "plastic"` call would otherwise stop at. `bikeData.rib.gz` ends `TransformEnd` with no trailing newline, which the tokenizer's end-of-input handling must not corrupt into `TransformEndd`, leaving that block unclosed (`tests/rib/nonewline/`'s defect); `bike.rib` reaches exit 0 and writes a non-empty image. `getRSPatch` wires `Patch` to `createParametric`, so the image reads as a bicycle rather than every pixel the background color (a separate defect `tests/patchnorender_test.cpp` pins). Used for the dedicated `ReadArchive`/gzip proof, not the corpus proof. |

Checked in as gzip'd/plain exactly as fetched; `bikeData.rib.gz` is not
decompressed in-tree so the gzip-transparency test has a real gzip'd file to
exercise, not a synthetic one.
